import{_ as a,o as r,c as t,a2 as i}from"./chunks/framework.Bx6eOp9J.js";const h=JSON.parse('{"title":"Agent Research & Competitor Intelligence · 2026-11-09","description":"Catch-up since November 5: async supervisors, durable tasks, enterprise authority, delivery evidence and competitor governance.","frontmatter":{"title":"Agent Research & Competitor Intelligence · 2026-11-09","description":"Catch-up since November 5: async supervisors, durable tasks, enterprise authority, delivery evidence and competitor governance.","outline":[2,3]},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/runtime/daily-intelligence/2026-11-09.md","filePath":"en/runtime/daily-intelligence/2026-11-09.md","lastUpdated":1794193904000}'),n={name:"en/runtime/daily-intelligence/2026-11-09.md"};function s(o,e,l,c,d,p){return r(),t("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[i('<h1 id="agent-research-competitor-intelligence-·-2026-11-09" tabindex="-1">Agent Research &amp; Competitor Intelligence · 2026-11-09 <a class="header-anchor" href="#agent-research-competitor-intelligence-·-2026-11-09" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Agent Research &amp; Competitor Intelligence · 2026-11-09&quot;">​</a></h1><p>The central finding is a shift from parallel Agent calls toward responsive supervisors, durable background tasks, traceable authority and evidence-backed completion. Deep Agents&#39; async reference deserves priority experience; Paperclip&#39;s native Runner advances delivery; Paradev&#39;s cloud-to-host access and Palmier&#39;s auto-approval direction need separate enterprise-governance assessment.</p><p>This report is archived under the actual execution date, <strong>2026-11-09 (Asia/Shanghai)</strong>, catching up since the last successful monitor without fabricating November 6–8 daily reports. Execution began at 09:21:57 Shanghai (01:21:57 UTC); most complete commit-query baselines were November 5 at 05:43:53 UTC, with per-source local successful records authoritative. Event dates are UTC from original sources unless stated otherwise. Orkas #193 was verified during execution after the initial cutoff and is labelled separately. OPEN→MERGED and main→release are delivery/status advances, not newly invented mechanisms.</p><p>There are <strong>54 observations and 8 analyses</strong>. This is source collection and classification: no local product experiments, articles, formal development reviews or actual comments were completed.</p><p><strong>10:25 scheduled incremental check:</strong> execution began 2026-11-09 at 02:25:34 UTC, continuing per-source successful windows. O33–O54 and A6–A8 are added; all initial 32 observations/five analyses remain. Today&#39;s total is 54 observations/eight analyses. PR/issue states were rechecked at 02:38 UTC; #60326 appeared after the start. Previously reported #193 is not counted again. Recent-release sampling found no new releases relative to the first run; main merges and OPEN proposals are not presented as released capabilities.</p><h2 id="layer-1-factual-observations" tabindex="-1">Layer 1: factual observations <a class="header-anchor" href="#layer-1-factual-observations" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Layer 1: factual observations&quot;">​</a></h2><h3 id="o1-yue-zhao-s-audit-catalogue-merges-four-external-contributions" tabindex="-1">O1 Yue Zhao&#39;s audit catalogue merges four external contributions <a class="header-anchor" href="#o1-yue-zhao-s-audit-catalogue-merges-four-external-contributions" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O1 Yue Zhao&#39;s audit catalogue merges four external contributions&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>MERGED · 2026-11-05 UTC</strong></p><p>Yue Zhao&#39;s awesome-auditable-ai merged #47–50: Agent Role Contracts checks declared roles, task scopes, review separation and handoffs; Ponder uses an append-only JSONL ledger for review coverage/convergence; Tracekit moves decisions and signing into a separate process; SealedRun joins decision-record tools. Acceptance of contributed entries does not establish Zhao&#39;s authorship or verification of every claim. ARC does not grant runtime authority; Tracekit observes only mediated calls and its same-user development signer lacks strong isolation. SealedRun 0.8.0 was released November 1; the new event is catalogue inclusion. Classify as reference material and source-review candidates, not installed tools. Discussion: where do declaration checks, runtime authority and offline evidence verification each stop?</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/yzhao062/awesome-auditable-ai/pull/47" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a> · <a href="https://github.com/yzhao062/awesome-auditable-ai/pull/48" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">2</a> · <a href="https://github.com/yzhao062/awesome-auditable-ai/pull/49" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">3</a> · <a href="https://github.com/yzhao062/awesome-auditable-ai/pull/50" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">4</a></p><h3 id="o2-agents-sdk-application-owned-persistence-and-bounded-strict-executors" tabindex="-1">O2 Agents SDK: application-owned persistence and bounded strict executors <a class="header-anchor" href="#o2-agents-sdk-application-owned-persistence-and-bounded-strict-executors" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O2 Agents SDK: application-owned persistence and bounded strict executors&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>PRERELEASE · 2026-11-08 UTC</strong></p><p>Python v0.24.0-alpha.7 and JS v0.22.0-alpha.2 ship preview packages with native application-owned SessionStore and explicit unknown-outcome reconciliation. Initial persistence covers root function tools, not every child agent, MCP/manual tool or portable checkpoint; Python&#39;s SQLite reference targets single-host local POSIX. New environments default to strict with compatible executors; Windows/remote legacy requires explicit selection, and restoring old profiles does not upgrade their policy. This advances November 5 main-branch observations into prereleases. Learning: publish recovery/platform limits explicitly. Candidate: crash recovery with uncertain side effects; not executed.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/openai/openai-agents-python/releases/tag/v0.24.0-alpha.7" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a> · <a href="https://github.com/openai/openai-agents-js/releases/tag/v0.22.0-alpha.2" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">2</a></p><h3 id="o3-agents-sdk-budgets-supervision-and-a2a-retain-application-duties" tabindex="-1">O3 Agents SDK: budgets, supervision and A2A retain application duties <a class="header-anchor" href="#o3-agents-sdk-budgets-supervision-and-a2a-retain-application-duties" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O3 Agents SDK: budgets, supervision and A2A retain application duties&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>PRERELEASE · 2026-11-08 UTC</strong></p><p>The same native previews include RunBudget, before_complete review, context transforms, an A2A 1.0 polling adapter and cancellation cleanup. Budgets gate subsequent root request admission rather than imposing a hard spend ceiling; admitted requests may exceed the budget and child coverage is limited. Rejected completion text may already have streamed and remain in history, so review is not a pre-disclosure barrier. Applications still own current A2A authorization, durable storage and unknown mutation outcomes; cancellation does not undo external effects. Classify as protocol-adapter/Runtime governance experiment candidates, not automatic FCoP kernel requirements. Discussion: correlate admission, approval and uncertain outcomes in auditable events.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/openai/openai-agents-python/releases/tag/v0.24.0-alpha.7" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a> · <a href="https://github.com/openai/openai-agents-js/releases/tag/v0.22.0-alpha.2" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">2</a></p><h3 id="o4-codex-0-167-ships-session-continuity-and-approval-lifecycle-changes" tabindex="-1">O4 Codex 0.167 ships session continuity and approval lifecycle changes <a class="header-anchor" href="#o4-codex-0-167-ships-session-continuity-and-approval-lifecycle-changes" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O4 Codex 0.167 ships session continuity and approval lifecycle changes&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>RELEASED · 2026-11-06 UTC</strong></p><p>Stable rust-v0.167.0 adds a codex agents --no-daemon command centre and unread state. Chats remain readable across provider changes; when resumption is unavailable an editable prompt can start a new chat. Network approvals close with their request and are serialized per endpoint; compaction accounting includes failed responses and missing usage. The changelog also lists fresh human confirmation for managed prompt-user and turn-scoped tools. These distinguish visibility, resumability and restart preparation; they do not prove repair of this automation&#39;s remote routing failure. Classify as continued observation and recovery experience candidates.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/openai/codex/releases/tag/rust-v0.167.0" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a></p><h3 id="o5-codex-main-separates-project-write-access-from-project-trust" tabindex="-1">O5 Codex main separates project write access from project trust <a class="header-anchor" href="#o5-codex-main-separates-project-write-access-from-project-trust" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O5 Codex main separates project write access from project trust&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>MERGED · 2026-11-07 UTC</strong></p><p>#60041 requires explicit project trust in non-interactive execution: workspace-write is not a proxy for trust, untrusted project configuration/MCP is disabled, explicit trust persists even in ephemeral mode, and explicit distrust is not casually overridden. #60046 derives MCP network policy and preserves valid denials: denials are published before persistence, while grants wait for successful storage. Both merged after 0.167 was released; inclusion in that stable release is not claimed. Classify as enterprise authorization research. Discussion: failure ordering among trust configuration, persistence and immediate revocation.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/openai/codex/pull/60041" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a> · <a href="https://github.com/openai/codex/pull/60046" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">2</a></p><h3 id="o6-claude-code-makes-unattended-refusal-visible-in-exit-results" tabindex="-1">O6 Claude Code makes unattended refusal visible in exit results <a class="header-anchor" href="#o6-claude-code-makes-unattended-refusal-visible-in-exit-results" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O6 Claude Code makes unattended refusal visible in exit results&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>RELEASED · 2026-11-06 UTC</strong></p><p>In v2.1.319, auto mode asks or denies calls it cannot check instead of silently falling back to a local classifier, subject to provider/configuration exceptions. permissions.denyInsteadOfPrompting lets unattended workflows deny and continue. claude -p returns nonzero when refusal prevents completion; resumption preserves missing deferred MCP calls and refuses subsequent input. Background MCP tasks return before notification; cloud /loop lifetime remains container-bound. Learning: define automatic execution, continuation, failure and background lifetime separately. Classify as continued observation and refusal-propagation testing candidates.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/anthropics/claude-code/releases/tag/v2.1.319" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a></p><h3 id="o7-adk-2-14-clarifies-environment-skill-execution-and-evaluation-interfaces" tabindex="-1">O7 ADK 2.14 clarifies environment, skill execution and evaluation interfaces <a class="header-anchor" href="#o7-adk-2-14-clarifies-environment-skill-execution-and-evaluation-interfaces" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O7 ADK 2.14 clarifies environment, skill execution and evaluation interfaces&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>RELEASED · 2026-11-07 UTC</strong></p><p>v2.14.0 was actually published November 7 UTC although its note heading says November 4. It stabilizes environment/A2A interfaces, adds chunked file reading and validated skill-script execution, and introduces MultiRunConfig, parallel evaluation repeats and configurable ToolArgsMatcher. Passing Enum members to tools is a compatibility change. Upstream reports 23% faster and 9% less memory on a 30-node/200-turn case; this was not reproduced locally or generalized. Classify interfaces/evaluation as references and skill execution/argument migration as experiment candidates.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/google/adk-python/releases/tag/v2.14.0" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a></p><h3 id="o8-adk-main-fixes-approval-arguments-resumption-and-shared-tool-lifetime" tabindex="-1">O8 ADK main fixes approval arguments, resumption and shared-tool lifetime <a class="header-anchor" href="#o8-adk-main-fixes-approval-arguments-resumption-and-shared-tool-lifetime" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O8 ADK main fixes approval arguments, resumption and shared-tool lifetime&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>MAIN COMMITS · 2026-11-06–07 UTC</strong></p><p>669f55b repairs confirmation predicates affected by Enum conversion: ordinary predicates retain raw enum values while tool functions still receive members, with a separate validated-argument path. ba142cf avoids rerunning already handled input when resuming wait_for_output unless rerun_on_resume explicitly requests it. d0f227a reference-counts shared toolsets so only the last holder closes them. These main commits were inspected; their individual inclusion in 2.14 was not established. Classify as Runtime recovery/authorization experiment candidates, without inferring matching CodeFlowMu defects.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/google/adk-python/commit/669f55bbaa75f49e59a2273c2ecbb534749c85b0" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a> · <a href="https://github.com/google/adk-python/commit/ba142cf8e91b045aa0b435f71dbd7c3e686efa45" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">2</a> · <a href="https://github.com/google/adk-python/commit/d0f227afc3c7187de875dda2e11ff4b47fe285cd" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">3</a></p><h3 id="o9-adk-evaluation-excludes-thought-text-from-delivered-answers" tabindex="-1">O9 ADK evaluation excludes thought text from delivered answers <a class="header-anchor" href="#o9-adk-evaluation-excludes-thought-text-from-delivered-answers" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O9 ADK evaluation excludes thought text from delivered answers&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>MAIN COMMIT · 2026-11-07 UTC</strong></p><p>dd7161f removes model thoughts from relevant evaluation input so judges assess answers/visible text; it does not uniformly rewrite Vertex&#39;s structured multi-turn path. The useful lesson is to fix the evaluated object: internal reasoning, tool traces and delivered artifacts are different. Main commit verified; release inclusion and local score impact remain unverified. Classify as evaluation-method experiments. Discussion: retain trajectory analysis and delivery grading as separately labelled measurements.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/google/adk-python/commit/dd7161f7a2b88cd7ae482cc2acd8423675ac027c" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a></p><h3 id="o10-langgraph-exposes-strict-resume-through-the-python-sdk" tabindex="-1">O10 LangGraph exposes strict_resume through the Python SDK <a class="header-anchor" href="#o10-langgraph-exposes-strict-resume-through-the-python-sdk" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O10 LangGraph exposes strict_resume through the Python SDK&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>MERGED + RELEASED · 2026-11-05–06 UTC</strong></p><p>#9599 ships in sdk==0.4.14, exposing strict_resume on synchronous/asynchronous create, stream and wait for thread runs. Stateless calls omit the field and older servers may ignore it. A strict client request therefore does not establish server-side enforcement without version/capability checks. This advances the November 5 private-field observation into public SDK delivery. Classify as recovery-contract experience candidates; map first to Runtime/adapters rather than automatically changing FCoP&#39;s kernel.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/langchain-ai/langgraph/pull/9599" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a> · <a href="https://github.com/langchain-ai/langgraph/releases/tag/sdk%3D%3D0.4.14" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">2</a></p><h3 id="o11-mcp-repairs-oneof-example-type-validation-boundaries" tabindex="-1">O11 MCP repairs oneOf example type-validation boundaries <a class="header-anchor" href="#o11-mcp-repairs-oneof-example-type-validation-boundaries" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O11 MCP repairs oneOf example type-validation boundaries&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>MERGED · 2026-11-06 UTC</strong></p><p>#3185 fixes documentation/schema examples where branch-local properties in oneOf could bypass intended type checks. Common properties move to the root and required sets express exclusive branches. This is an example correction, not a universal security patch across MCP SDKs or a new wire capability. Classify as protocol/schema reference material and counterexample-test candidates: state/message contracts need negative cases.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/modelcontextprotocol/pull/3185" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a></p><h3 id="o12-a2ui-and-ag2-demonstrate-wire-differences-behind-similar-version-labels" tabindex="-1">O12 A2UI and AG2 demonstrate wire differences behind similar version labels <a class="header-anchor" href="#o12-a2ui-and-ag2-demonstrate-wire-differences-behind-similar-version-labels" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O12 A2UI and AG2 demonstrate wire differences behind similar version labels&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>MERGED · 2026-11-06 / 08 UTC</strong></p><p>A2UI #3999 marks 1.0 current/stable and deprecates 0.8. AG2 #3618 follows the stable wire: RC v1.0 becomes 1.0, with TextField.value, capability-key and MIME corrections. AG2 still defaults to 0.9; known A2A capability-scoping limits remain, and a new AG2 release containing these changes was not verified. Learning: test version negotiation, catalogues and capabilities together. This is UI/adapter interoperability research, not evidence of a same-layer FCoP competitor.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/a2ui-project/a2ui/pull/3999" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a> · <a href="https://github.com/ag2ai/ag2/pull/3618" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">2</a></p><h3 id="o13-dspy-distinguishes-reusable-history-from-interpreter-state-restoration" tabindex="-1">O13 DSPy distinguishes reusable history from interpreter-state restoration <a class="header-anchor" href="#o13-dspy-distinguishes-reusable-history-from-interpreter-state-restoration" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O13 DSPy distinguishes reusable history from interpreter-state restoration&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>MERGED · 2026-11-08 UTC</strong></p><p>#10827 adds reusable dspy.History conversations to RLM. Each invocation still creates a fresh interpreter; earlier code is replayed as text rather than executed and Python variables are not restored. Current trajectory is separate from a compactable history copy. LocalInterpreter trusts its host and is not a substitute for a Deno/Pyodide security sandbox. Main merged; stable delivery unverified. Classify as memory/recovery research, comparing conversational continuity with actual execution-state continuity.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/stanfordnlp/dspy/pull/10827" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a></p><h3 id="o14-crewai-exposes-script-and-evaluation-failures-explicitly" tabindex="-1">O14 CrewAI exposes script and evaluation failures explicitly <a class="header-anchor" href="#o14-crewai-exposes-script-and-evaluation-failures-explicitly" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O14 CrewAI exposes script and evaluation failures explicitly&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>RELEASED · 2026-11-08 UTC</strong></p><p>1.15.38 returns nonzero on project-script failure, records evaluation errors as run_failed, adds per-evaluation usage/target spans and restricts .env tracing permissions. 1.15.39 exists later, but its documentation increment is not counted as another governance capability. Learning: external schedulers need failure-versus-completion signals rather than process termination alone; comprehensive failure semantics are not established. Classify as failure-propagation/observability references, without local reproduction.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/crewAIInc/crewAI/releases/tag/1.15.38" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a></p><h3 id="o15-paperclip-releases-native-runner-with-platform-and-migration-limits" tabindex="-1">O15 Paperclip releases native Runner with platform and migration limits <a class="header-anchor" href="#o15-paperclip-releases-native-runner-with-platform-and-migration-limits" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O15 Paperclip releases native Runner with platform and migration limits&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>RELEASED · 2026-11-06 UTC</strong></p><p>v2026.1106.0 defaults qualified new Claude/Codex/OpenCode/Pi agents to native Runner on supported macOS/Linux; Linux requires glibc&gt;=2.39. Existing agents retain settings and can opt into upgrade; Windows default support is not established. The release removes experimental Pipelines surfaces/implementation while retaining tables and Cases/Routines. Sentry run-failure reporting becomes explicit opt-in. Its 515 inter-release commits are not all new events in this run. Competitor learning/risk: high/high; raise native-delivery maturity with platform/migration limits. Experience candidate: new/existing employee upgrades.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/paperclipai/paperclip/releases/tag/v2026.1106.0" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a></p><h3 id="o16-paperclip-makes-skill-versions-telemetry-and-restore-points-operational-choices" tabindex="-1">O16 Paperclip makes skill versions, telemetry and restore points operational choices <a class="header-anchor" href="#o16-paperclip-makes-skill-versions-telemetry-and-restore-points-operational-choices" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O16 Paperclip makes skill versions, telemetry and restore points operational choices&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>RELEASED · 2026-11-06 UTC</strong></p><p>The same stable release adds repository-derived skill sources, roughly five-minute refresh, per-run version pinning and disconnection choices. Its model-based injection classifier requires configuration/opt-in and is not a default security guarantee. Telemetry is off by default, with OTLP/company credentials available. Workspace compaction/expiry uses encrypted restore points; an expired point can lead to a fresh workspace with an explanation, not exact restoration. Competitor learning/risk: high/high; maintain close watch. Study whether skill versions, evidence and recovery semantics can be traced together. Classify as operational-mechanism experience candidates.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/paperclipai/paperclip/releases/tag/v2026.1106.0" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a></p><h3 id="o17-paperclip-tightens-task-ownership-and-completion-races" tabindex="-1">O17 Paperclip tightens task ownership and completion races <a class="header-anchor" href="#o17-paperclip-tightens-task-ownership-and-completion-races" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O17 Paperclip tightens task ownership and completion races&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>MERGED · 2026-11-05 / 08 UTC</strong></p><p>#19186 advances from OPEN to MERGED: a reassigned-away run cannot close the new owner&#39;s task, while its result remains in history. #20108 waits up to 30 seconds for earlier held tool calls before judging finish/block, rejecting settlement when calls remain pending or results unseen. The latter postdates the November 6 release; the former&#39;s individual release inclusion was not checked. Competitor learning/risk: high/high; maintain close watch. Study who judges completion against ownership and unresolved evidence; this is not proof of global exactly-once execution.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/paperclipai/paperclip/pull/19186" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a> · <a href="https://github.com/paperclipai/paperclip/pull/20108" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">2</a></p><h3 id="o18-paradev-preserves-resumed-permissions-and-opens-an-explicit-cloud-to-host-boundary" tabindex="-1">O18 Paradev preserves resumed permissions and opens an explicit cloud-to-host boundary <a class="header-anchor" href="#o18-paradev-preserves-resumed-permissions-and-opens-an-explicit-cloud-to-host-boundary" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O18 Paradev preserves resumed permissions and opens an explicit cloud-to-host boundary&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>RELEASED + MERGED · 2026-11-06–08 UTC</strong></p><p>Verified Superset successor Paradev desktop-v2.0.6 includes permission-mode preservation across resume/fork/recovery (#9354) and owner-opened host access for owned cloud workspaces (#9408/#9409). The toggle is off by default and owner-only; requests recheck API authorization, boxes cannot enable it themselves, and WebSockets are rejected. Once enabled, owner-box code can drive the host as owner; the PR has no second host-side lock, so owner-only is not code safety. Removing Beta labels does not verify maturity. Competitor learning high, cross-host risk high; raise trust-boundary scrutiny. Candidates: revocation, injection and resumed permissions.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/ParadevAI/Paradev/releases/tag/desktop-v2.0.6" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a> · <a href="https://github.com/ParadevAI/Paradev/pull/9408" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">2</a> · <a href="https://github.com/ParadevAI/Paradev/pull/9354" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">3</a></p><h3 id="o19-palmier-improves-continuity-while-explicitly-adopting-auto-approval" tabindex="-1">O19 Palmier improves continuity while explicitly adopting auto-approval <a class="header-anchor" href="#o19-palmier-improves-continuity-while-explicitly-adopting-auto-approval" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O19 Palmier improves continuity while explicitly adopting auto-approval&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>RELEASED / DEFAULT-BRANCH COMMITS · 2026-11-06–09 UTC</strong></p><p>Commits around v1.1.26–28 force YOLO/auto-approval and remove the per-task approval path: a governance direction change, not stronger enterprise safety. need-input can end a CLI turn while retaining the Palmier run/browser and resume the same session; adapters unable to resume context are removed, and host standing prompts enter launches including scheduled tasks. NATS reconnect cleanup avoids duplicate RPC and a pending-run marker claims the exact run rather than guessing the newest. Competitor continuity learning is high, enterprise approval fit decreases, and risk scrutiny rises. These mechanisms still need independent validation and do not prove end-to-end duplicate-effect prevention.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/caihongxu/palmier/releases/tag/v1.1.28" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a> · <a href="https://github.com/caihongxu/palmier/commit/2e3c584fc97dcab37f0a20d17441f998cdeb9c8a" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">2</a> · <a href="https://github.com/caihongxu/palmier/commit/fe83ec62a2e97822e503b58eebe35695805fe71f" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">3</a> · <a href="https://github.com/caihongxu/palmier/commit/cf6b69d3308f116e38a949ce08dfbe97fe23e556" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">4</a></p><h3 id="o20-orca-strengthens-mobile-offline-control-and-pairing-identity" tabindex="-1">O20 Orca strengthens mobile offline control and pairing identity <a class="header-anchor" href="#o20-orca-strengthens-mobile-offline-control-and-pairing-identity" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O20 Orca strengthens mobile offline control and pairing identity&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>RELEASED · 2026-11-08 UTC</strong></p><p>v1.4.254 adds ordered mobile offline queues and preserves drafts, attachments, file edits and automation drafts across app termination. One-use pairing codes require the device to sign into its own identity; legacy-code compatibility remains marked. orca serve restricts public addresses by default with LAN/Tailscale exceptions, and new-repository trust differs from grandfathered repositories. Release notes do not establish exactly-once offline command execution. Competitor learning/risk: high/high; maintain close watch. Candidates: weak-network approval/pause/resume and identity revocation. Commit coverage stops at 400 records and remains incomplete.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/stablyai/orca/releases/tag/v1.4.254" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a></p><h3 id="o21-agentlas-update-cleanup-preserves-active-versions-and-rollback-room" tabindex="-1">O21 Agentlas update cleanup preserves active versions and rollback room <a class="header-anchor" href="#o21-agentlas-update-cleanup-preserves-active-versions-and-rollback-room" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O21 Agentlas update cleanup preserves active versions and rollback room&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>RELEASED · 2026-11-06–08 UTC</strong></p><p>os-v1.2.87 cleanup retains current, host-configured, running/recently used runtimes, rollback candidates and newer versions after manual rollback. Unknown usage prevents deletion, at most five versions are removed per run, and cleanup failure does not fail the update. .88 excludes failed translation batches from daily quota with bounded retries; .89 improves browser error handling. This is not comprehensive disaster recovery. Competitor learning high/risk medium-high; keep operational delivery under observation. Candidates: updating and rolling back with active workers and disk pressure, not version-count-based maturity.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/agentlas-ai/Agentlas-OS/releases/tag/os-v1.2.87" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a> · <a href="https://github.com/agentlas-ai/Agentlas-OS/releases/tag/os-v1.2.88" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">2</a> · <a href="https://github.com/agentlas-ai/Agentlas-OS/releases/tag/os-v1.2.89" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">3</a></p><h3 id="o22-openhands-ships-service-identity-and-explicit-credentials-for-automation" tabindex="-1">O22 OpenHands ships service identity and explicit credentials for automation <a class="header-anchor" href="#o22-openhands-ships-service-identity-and-explicit-credentials-for-automation" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O22 OpenHands ships service identity and explicit credentials for automation&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>RELEASED · 2026-11-05–06 UTC</strong></p><p>Canvas v1.36.0 lists service-account automations (#19033) and enterprise Git template/provider setup. SDK 1.60.0–1.60.2 includes explicitly approved secrets for Canvas backends, encryption of bash event files at rest and a large remote-conversation-state fix. Ordinary interface changes in v1.37.0 are not counted as another governance breakthrough. Service identity opens research into responsibility/credential lifecycle; tenant scopes, rotation and revocation still need checking. Feature titles do not establish full enterprise isolation. Classify as identity/recovery experience candidates. As a competitor, learning high/risk medium-high; maintain assessment.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/OpenHands/OpenHands/releases/tag/v1.36.0" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a> · <a href="https://github.com/OpenHands/software-agent-sdk/releases/tag/v1.60.0" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">2</a> · <a href="https://github.com/OpenHands/software-agent-sdk/releases/tag/v1.60.2" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">3</a> · <a href="https://github.com/OpenHands/OpenHands/pull/19033" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">4</a></p><h3 id="o23-orkas-isolates-unreadable-skills-and-preserves-final-execution-records" tabindex="-1">O23 Orkas isolates unreadable skills and preserves final execution records <a class="header-anchor" href="#o23-orkas-isolates-unreadable-skills-and-preserves-final-execution-records" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O23 Orkas isolates unreadable skills and preserves final execution records&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>MERGED · 2026-11-08–09 UTC</strong></p><p>#190 skips only an unreadable Skill, supports retry after repair and avoids persisting an incomplete catalogue as trusted state; root-directory failure stays explicit. #193 flushes a final Codex JSON-RPC record lacking a newline before stdout closes, retaining failure/interruption/provenance. It merged about one minute after this run&#39;s start and is separately labelled an event discovered during catch-up, not inserted into the earlier cutoff. Both are main changes without verified new stable delivery; Windows GUI/paid models were not tested. Competitor learning medium-high/risk medium; maintain radar. Study partial degradation without losing terminal evidence.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/Orkas-AI/Orkas/pull/190" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a> · <a href="https://github.com/Orkas-AI/Orkas/pull/193" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">2</a></p><h3 id="o24-langfuse-moves-read-export-auditing-into-stable-delivery" tabindex="-1">O24 Langfuse moves read/export auditing into stable delivery <a class="header-anchor" href="#o24-langfuse-moves-read-export-auditing-into-stable-delivery" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O24 Langfuse moves read/export auditing into stable delivery&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>RELEASED · 2026-11-05 UTC</strong></p><p>v4.76.0 delivers previously observed read-access auditing and prompt-cap rollback alongside export-download auditing and self-hosted gateway compose. Existing EE/opt-in and route-coverage limits remain; universal read auditing is not established. The new value is stable delivery and download evidence, not reinvention of earlier main changes. Classify as enterprise-audit references. Discussion: correlate reading, export, downloading and post-revocation access in one evidence chain.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/langfuse/langfuse/releases/tag/v4.76.0" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a></p><h3 id="o25-github-mcp-has-open-reports-on-read-only-switches-and-temporary-urls" tabindex="-1">O25 GitHub MCP has open reports on read-only switches and temporary URLs <a class="header-anchor" href="#o25-github-mcp-has-open-reports-on-read-only-switches-and-temporary-urls" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O25 GitHub MCP has open reports on read-only switches and temporary URLs&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>OPEN ISSUES · 2026-11-08 UTC</strong></p><p>#3747 reports that v2.1.0 parses GITHUB_READ_ONLY=yes/on and similar strings as false, leaving write tools exposed unlike 1/true. This is a reporter claim without local reproduction or a verified released fix. #3748 reports short-lived tokenized private-repository download URLs in directory results; its author frames it as low-severity hardening, not an established vulnerability, with legitimate large-file download needs. Similar LOCKDOWN claims remain unverified. Classify as configuration/context-exposure experiment candidates. Discussion: fail startup on invalid safety settings and minimize model-visible URLs.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/github/github-mcp-server/issues/3747" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a> · <a href="https://github.com/github/github-mcp-server/issues/3748" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">2</a></p><h3 id="o26-swe-bench-proposes-refusing-unevaluable-empty-results" tabindex="-1">O26 SWE-bench proposes refusing unevaluable empty results <a class="header-anchor" href="#o26-swe-bench-proposes-refusing-unevaluable-empty-results" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O26 SWE-bench proposes refusing unevaluable empty results&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>OPEN PR · 2026-11-08 UTC</strong></p><p>#718 proposes refusing grading when a suite exits nonzero with an empty recorded-failure map, preventing FAIL_ONLY from treating absent results as success. The PR describes 25 upstream tests; it remains OPEN and was not reproduced locally. This does not invalidate all existing SWE-bench scores. Classify as independent-verification/failure-taxonomy experiments. Digital-employee value: separate absence of recorded failure from evidence of completion, without creating a CodeFlowMu development task.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/SWE-bench/SWE-bench/pull/718" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a></p><h3 id="o27-staffdeck-report-exposes-a-possible-sop-tool-output-mismatch" tabindex="-1">O27 StaffDeck report exposes a possible SOP/tool-output mismatch <a class="header-anchor" href="#o27-staffdeck-report-exposes-a-possible-sop-tool-output-mismatch" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O27 StaffDeck report exposes a possible SOP/tool-output mismatch&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>OPEN ISSUE · 2026-11-07 UTC</strong></p><p>#308 reports an SOP requiring per-item comments and URLs while its bound tool returns task-level summary fields; capability_refs filtering can leave textual instructions inconsistent with available actions. The reporter describes results after changing bindings/action nodes; these are not local experiments or proof of an official static contract validator. Competitor learning high, risk pending reproduction; add SOP executability scrutiny without downgrading the whole product. Classify as an experiment candidate: check input/output/action/acceptance-field consistency when saving SOPs.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/OpenBMB/StaffDeck/issues/308" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a></p><h3 id="o28-mcp-apps-report-separates-acknowledgement-from-model-delivery" tabindex="-1">O28 MCP Apps report separates acknowledgement from model delivery <a class="header-anchor" href="#o28-mcp-apps-report-separates-acknowledgement-from-model-delivery" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O28 MCP Apps report separates acknowledgement from model delivery&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>OPEN ISSUE · 2026-11-07 UTC</strong></p><p>ext-apps #852 reports advertised ui/update-model-context capability and successful acknowledgements without an observable indication that updates reach the model, with reporter-observed Web versus desktop/Android differences. SHOULD/MAY language and deferred behavior need separating. A fault across all hosts and a released fix are not established. Classify as protocol-observability tracking and cross-host experience candidates: distinguish receipt, queuing, model consumption and user-visible delivery instead of equating ACK with delivery.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/ext-apps/issues/852" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a></p><h3 id="o29-cursor-adds-voice-access-alongside-background-agent-work" tabindex="-1">O29 Cursor adds voice access alongside background agent work <a class="header-anchor" href="#o29-cursor-adds-voice-access-alongside-background-agent-work" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O29 Cursor adds voice access alongside background agent work&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>OFFICIAL PRODUCT CHANGELOG · 2026-11-06</strong></p><p>The official Voice Chat with Agents update describes talking to a voice agent while coding agents work in the background, across IDE/window/Web/iOS entry points, with short progress updates and full text serving different needs. This verifies the official product description, not tested cross-device state, approval or interruption consistency. Research value is an accessible supervisory interface for digital employees rather than merely shrinking desktop UI. Competitor learning/risk medium-high; retain product-layer observation, not a same-layer FCoP comparison.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://cursor.com/changelog/voice-chat" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a></p><h3 id="o30-deep-agents-async-supervisor-worker-threads-are-a-priority-experience-candidate" tabindex="-1">O30 Deep Agents async supervisor/worker threads are a priority experience candidate <a class="header-anchor" href="#o30-deep-agents-async-supervisor-worker-threads-are-a-priority-experience-candidate" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O30 Deep Agents async supervisor/worker threads are a priority experience candidate&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>OFFICIAL ARTICLE + REFERENCE SOURCE · 2026-11-05</strong></p><p>The official article describes a responsive supervisor and background workers on independent threads, with start/check/update/cancel/list operations, interrupt-or-enqueue steering and approval-aware notification. The async-deep-agents reference uses LangGraph thread/run APIs, a persistent job registry and ASGI/HTTP: Runtime coordination, not a proven same-layer file protocol competitor to FCoP. Repository tool names differ from newer docs, notification wiring requires enabling, and notifications remain beta. Requirements include Python 3.11+/uv or Node 20+/pnpm, model credentials and worker slots. README clone naming is stale and no explicit LICENSE was found; verify before adoption. Not installed. High learning value and a priority experience candidate. Discussion: durable jobs after compaction and reliable notifications during approval.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://www.langchain.com/blog/agents-are-going-async" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a> · <a href="https://github.com/langchain-ai/async-deep-agents/blob/main/README.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">2</a></p><h3 id="o31-deep-agents-releases-explicit-compaction-refusal-and-checkpoint-evidence" tabindex="-1">O31 Deep Agents releases explicit compaction/refusal and checkpoint evidence <a class="header-anchor" href="#o31-deep-agents-releases-explicit-compaction-refusal-and-checkpoint-evidence" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O31 Deep Agents releases explicit compaction/refusal and checkpoint evidence&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>RELEASED · 2026-11-05 UTC</strong></p><p>deepagents==0.7.33 raises model refusals by default, reports child refusals to parents, records task-call IDs on child checkpoints and adds async notification metadata. Failed compaction receives an explicit error status. 0.7.34 anchors summarization cutoffs by message ID. These are released failure-propagation/causal-link mechanisms, not proof of universally reliable async notification or recovery, nor repair of Codex&#39;s remote compaction service. Classify as failure-visibility/task-evidence experiments; no local runtime results.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/langchain-ai/deepagents/releases/tag/deepagents%3D%3D0.7.33" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a> · <a href="https://github.com/langchain-ai/deepagents/releases/tag/deepagents%3D%3D0.7.34" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">2</a></p><h3 id="o32-cursor-connects-code-generation-to-shareable-apps-and-publication-gates" tabindex="-1">O32 Cursor connects code generation to shareable apps and publication gates <a class="header-anchor" href="#o32-cursor-connects-code-generation-to-shareable-apps-and-publication-gates" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O32 Cursor connects code generation to shareable apps and publication gates&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>OFFICIAL PRODUCT CHANGELOG · 2026-11-05</strong></p><p>The Build Live Apps announcement makes app creation, Postgres, Cursor SSO authentication and hosting available on paid plans with cloud agents enabled, using Cursor Origin/GitHub repositories. Publication requires deterministic/agentic security checks and explicit user publication. Team admins can add rules, approve blocked publishes and unpublish apps. This verifies official claims, not tested check coverage, override auditing or rollback. The source provides a date without exact time: this is previously unrecorded material first checked after the same-day baseline, not proof it was published after the previous run finished. Competitor learning/risk high/high; raise application-delivery attention. Study evidence from build through acceptance to authorized publication, including administrator exceptions.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://cursor.com/changelog/build-live-apps" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a></p><h3 id="o33-codex-includes-historical-failures-with-no-items-in-full-transcripts" tabindex="-1">O33 Codex includes historical failures with no items in full transcripts <a class="header-anchor" href="#o33-codex-includes-historical-failures-with-no-items-in-full-transcripts" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O33 Codex includes historical failures with no items in full transcripts&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>MERGED · 2026-11-09 01:54 UTC</strong></p><p>#60320 adds Cloud turn metadata in pages of 100 after item loading, deduplicating overlaps and preserving order so older itemless errors appear in full session transcripts. Item-only history could omit them. Session transcripts use FullTranscript while exports retain Complete/item hydration; export completeness does not automatically follow. Merged into main; no new stable delivery verified. Classify as failure-visibility references/experience candidates. Discussion: distinguish session, export and audit completeness.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/openai/codex/pull/60320" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a></p><h3 id="o34-paperclip-records-human-intent-behind-ceo-hiring-denial" tabindex="-1">O34 Paperclip records human intent behind CEO hiring denial <a class="header-anchor" href="#o34-paperclip-records-human-intent-behind-ceo-hiring-denial" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O34 Paperclip records human intent behind CEO hiring denial&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>MERGED #20041 / OPEN #20151 · 2026-11-09</strong></p><p>#20041 (merged 02:05 UTC) records actor/time in ceoHiringDeniedBy. With a recorded board denial, CEO role no longer bypasses hiring restrictions, self-escalates or creates agents through company import; explicit agents:create grants are removed. Migration matters: historical unrecorded false, absent fields and creation-time false can still allow CEO hiring, requiring a fresh explicit board denial. #20151 remains OPEN, proposing final board-only 403 responses with human remediation rather than alternate-route searching. No new release verified. Competitor learning/risk high/high; add authorization-intent/legacy-migration scrutiny without upgrading stable maturity. Classify as governance experiments. Discuss denial provenance, migration and revocation.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/paperclipai/paperclip/pull/20041" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a> · <a href="https://github.com/paperclipai/paperclip/pull/20151" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">2</a></p><h3 id="o35-paperclip-rolls-authority-back-only-for-proven-undelivered-dead-runner-attaches" tabindex="-1">O35 Paperclip rolls authority back only for proven-undelivered dead-runner attaches <a class="header-anchor" href="#o35-paperclip-rolls-authority-back-only-for-proven-undelivered-dead-runner-attaches" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O35 Paperclip rolls authority back only for proven-undelivered dead-runner attaches&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>MERGED · 2026-11-09 01:36 UTC</strong></p><p>#20146 handles a hard kill after warm-attach authority rotation but before connection. Rollback requires resume_dead_runner, matching identities, zero connections/events/delivery counts, only a pending run.attach, no issued credentials/adoption and a verified prior suspended epoch. Uncertain delivery still fails closed. Production guards were read; no local experiment or stable-release inclusion verified. Competitor learning/risk high/high; increase recovery research attention. This is conditional local rollback, not generic reversal of external effects. Classify as recovery experiments; discuss durable evidence of non-delivery.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/paperclipai/paperclip/pull/20146" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a></p><h3 id="o36-paperclip-isolates-evaluation-oracles-beyond-tool-level-allowlists" tabindex="-1">O36 Paperclip isolates evaluation oracles beyond tool-level allowlists <a class="header-anchor" href="#o36-paperclip-isolates-evaluation-oracles-beyond-tool-level-allowlists" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O36 Paperclip isolates evaluation oracles beyond tool-level allowlists&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>MERGED · 2026-11-09 02:10 UTC</strong></p><p>#20044 records an OpenCode agent reaching scenario YAML/graders through bash despite read/grep restrictions. Linux Landlock now hides harness/private evidence/compiled caches, CI probes fail closed and fixture names lose test identifiers. Scope is eligible Linux local native cells, explicitly excluding Codex (bubblewrap conflict), installed releases, legacy adapters and Daytona; this is not a universal product sandbox. Upstream tests/paid OpenCode evidence are not local experiments. Competitor learning/risk high/high; increase evaluation-integrity scrutiny. Classify as independent-verification experiments. Discuss oracle/cache/evidence isolation from the tested identity.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/paperclipai/paperclip/pull/20044" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a></p><h3 id="o37-paperclip-chaos-grading-accounts-for-child-retries-and-parent-stall-wakes" tabindex="-1">O37 Paperclip chaos grading accounts for child retries and parent stall wakes <a class="header-anchor" href="#o37-paperclip-chaos-grading-accounts-for-child-retries-and-parent-stall-wakes" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O37 Paperclip chaos grading accounts for child retries and parent stall wakes&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>MERGED · 2026-11-09 02:12 UTC</strong></p><p>#20147 repairs a harness that retried only the parent while a blocked child remained unrecovered. It retries the child through the board path, counts that intervention in grading, and checks product activity for one parent wake per stall. This tests existing mechanisms rather than shipping general autonomous recovery. Its 203 upstream passing tests do not establish success of every paid chaos scenario; local experiments remain zero. Competitor learning high/risk stays high; add separate grading of eventual success and intervention cost. Classify as evaluation references/experiments.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/paperclipai/paperclip/pull/20147" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a></p><h3 id="o38-beads-fences-concurrent-http-deletes-but-retains-a-cancellation-history-gap" tabindex="-1">O38 Beads fences concurrent HTTP deletes but retains a cancellation-history gap <a class="header-anchor" href="#o38-beads-fences-concurrent-http-deletes-but-retains-a-cancellation-history-gap" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O38 Beads fences concurrent HTTP deletes but retains a cancellation-history gap&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>MERGED · 2026-11-09 02:22 UTC</strong></p><p>#8227 routes bd serve guarded deletes to a per-issue GET_LOCK through provider wrappers, refusing unfenced execution when unreachable. Normal same-token races yield one success and other 404/CLI exit-13 results; writes retain policy/timing wrappers. Limits remain: lock/write sessions differ, so cancellation can still produce an extra delete-history commit; fence timeouts map to 500. This is not global exactly-once. Upstream real-Dolt comparisons are not local results. Learning high, task-ledger competition medium; increase consistency attention. Map to Runtime/storage adapters, not automatically FCoP kernel. Classify as concurrency/cancellation experiments.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/gastownhall/beads/pull/8227" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a></p><h3 id="o39-beads-tests-request-fields-and-actor-attribution-across-backends" tabindex="-1">O39 Beads tests request fields and actor attribution across backends <a class="header-anchor" href="#o39-beads-tests-request-fields-and-actor-attribution-across-backends" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O39 Beads tests request fields and actor attribution across backends&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>MERGED #8265 / #8267 · 2026-11-09 02:02–02:18 UTC</strong></p><p>#8265 checks request fields for contract cases and compares entire requests on plain-v0 forwarding. Of 342 fields, 301 have syntactic setter coverage and 41 reasoned shrink-only waivers; this does not prove semantic correctness. #8267 pins journal/lifecycle/delete-rewrite actors, but the HTTP journal leg remains skipped for a client Actor-copy defect; some actorless deletes/derived maintenance remain allowed. Both add tests, not fixes for every attribution defect. Learning high/competition medium; retain ledger/protocol observation. Classify as complementary FCoP contract-verification references/experiments. Discuss exposing skips as coverage gaps.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/gastownhall/beads/pull/8265" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a> · <a href="https://github.com/gastownhall/beads/pull/8267" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">2</a></p><h3 id="o40-orca-scopes-option-write-failure-without-automatically-killing-the-whole-process-group" tabindex="-1">O40 Orca scopes option-write failure without automatically killing the whole process group <a class="header-anchor" href="#o40-orca-scopes-option-write-failure-without-automatically-killing-the-whole-process-group" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O40 Orca scopes option-write failure without automatically killing the whole process group&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>MERGED · 2026-11-09 01:56 UTC</strong></p><p>#40038 confines unanswered model/effort changes to message failure, settles withdrawn writes before later prompt/compact admission and logs process generation, initiator and cause. Previously closing ACP stdin could yield OpenCode exit 0 followed by supervisor cleanup of its background process group; exit 0 did not establish task success. The author did not reproduce the original reporters&#39; trigger, only removed one Orca termination path and improved evidence. Main merged, release unverified. Learning/risk high/high; increase failure-isolation attention. Classify as lifecycle experiments.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/stablyai/orca/pull/40038" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a></p><h3 id="o41-orca-relaxes-terminal-stop-proof-with-explicit-availability-tradeoffs" tabindex="-1">O41 Orca relaxes terminal-stop proof with explicit availability tradeoffs <a class="header-anchor" href="#o41-orca-relaxes-terminal-stop-proof-with-explicit-availability-tradeoffs" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O41 Orca relaxes terminal-stop proof with explicit availability tradeoffs&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>MERGED · 2026-11-09 01:54 UTC</strong></p><p>#40033 extends Continue in Chat exit waits from 15 to 30 seconds and proceeds once waits settle even without proven clean exit, retaining foreground/hook checks. It explicitly accepts that a surviving old agent may evade the new-writer check after status rows are cleared. Missing/metadata-only history can start fresh; history in an unknown custom directory may also yield an empty chat without deleting old files or restoring context. Older paired hosts have limits. Learning/risk high/high; raise single-writer/handoff scrutiny rather than treating availability as stronger safety. Classify as dual-writer/custom-history experiments.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/stablyai/orca/pull/40033" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a></p><h3 id="o42-orca-classifies-16-codex-0-167-notifications-separately-from-chat-content" tabindex="-1">O42 Orca classifies 16 Codex 0.167 notifications separately from chat content <a class="header-anchor" href="#o42-orca-classifies-16-codex-0-167-notifications-separately-from-chat-content" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O42 Orca classifies 16 Codex 0.167 notifications separately from chat content&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>MERGED · 2026-11-09 01:45 UTC</strong></p><p>#39943 refreshes a notification list generated from 0.147, explicitly classifying 16 additions through 0.167 rather than falling back to substantive chat rows. Authorization recovery/queues/reverts become status signals; predicted text and some metadata are suppressed, while realtime completion retains substantive handling. Recovery failure still reaches users through turn errors. Upstream 0.162.1/0.167 tests were not reproduced locally. Learning medium-high/risk high; retain adapter scrutiny. Classify as event-contract references; discuss retaining audit status without model-visible prose.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/stablyai/orca/pull/39943" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a></p><h3 id="o43-github-mcp-proposes-fail-fast-handling-for-invalid-boolean-settings" tabindex="-1">O43 GitHub MCP proposes fail-fast handling for invalid boolean settings <a class="header-anchor" href="#o43-github-mcp-proposes-fail-fast-handling-for-invalid-boolean-settings" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O43 GitHub MCP proposes fail-fast handling for invalid boolean settings&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>OPEN · 2026-11-09 01:42 UTC</strong></p><p>#3750 responds to O25/#3747 by rejecting yes/on/typos/empty boolean environment values with nonzero startup, including read-only/lockdown settings. Explicit CLI flags still win and unset values remain false. This is a new OPEN proposal, not a merged/released resolution. The author reports binary/tests; no local reproduction. Classify as safety-configuration experiments. Discuss fail-fast settings, override priority and operational migration notices.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/github/github-mcp-server/pull/3750" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a></p><h3 id="o44-github-mcp-report-shows-successful-tools-can-still-lose-concurrent-field-updates" tabindex="-1">O44 GitHub MCP report shows successful tools can still lose concurrent field updates <a class="header-anchor" href="#o44-github-mcp-report-shows-successful-tools-can-still-lose-concurrent-field-updates" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O44 GitHub MCP report shows successful tools can still lose concurrent field updates&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>OPEN ISSUE · 2026-11-09 01:50 UTC</strong></p><p>#3751 reports issue_write reading a field snapshot, merging and PATCHing the entire set without freshness preconditions, overwriting concurrent edits; reading only 25 fields adds truncation risk. The reporter observed 19/30 lost concurrent pairs versus 0/28 per-field GraphQL controls. Not locally reproduced or a universal failure rate. Per-field mutations are suggested rather than snapshot replacement; no released fix verified. Classify as concurrent shared-artifact experiments. Discuss atomic field operations, conflict receipts and retry idempotency.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/github/github-mcp-server/issues/3751" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a></p><h3 id="o45-codex-request-separates-turn-completion-from-background-terminal-lifetime" tabindex="-1">O45 Codex request separates turn completion from background-terminal lifetime <a class="header-anchor" href="#o45-codex-request-separates-turn-completion-from-background-terminal-lifetime" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O45 Codex request separates turn completion from background-terminal lifetime&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>OPEN ISSUE · 2026-11-09 02:27 UTC</strong></p><p>#60326 reports a Windows CLI/terminal host remaining OSC7501 working while background development servers survive after the turn ends. It requests done/idle plus a separate background-terminal count. This is a reporter-specific host/CLI observation, not a locally reproduced universal fault or released fix. Created after this run&#39;s start, it is explicitly included as a subsequent verified discovery. Classify as supervisor/employee observability experiments. Discuss distinct turn/session/worker/background-job states.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/openai/codex/issues/60326" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a></p><h3 id="o46-mcp-machine-authentication-exposes-key-representation-compatibility-differences" tabindex="-1">O46 MCP machine authentication exposes key-representation compatibility differences <a class="header-anchor" href="#o46-mcp-machine-authentication-exposes-key-representation-compatibility-differences" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O46 MCP machine authentication exposes key-representation compatibility differences&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>OPEN · 2026-11-09 01:34 UTC</strong></p><p>TypeScript SDK #3165 proposes importing EdDSA/Ed25519 PKCS#8 PEM strings for private_key_jwt. The same key can sign as Uint8Array/JWK while the string path rejects it. none/unknown algorithms stay rejected; this is not unrestricted algorithm enablement. A changeset exists but the PR remains OPEN/unreleased. Upstream comparison tests were not run locally. Classify as enterprise machine-identity interoperability experiments, aligning documentation, serialization and token signing rather than upgrading the identity protocol.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/typescript-sdk/pull/3165" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a></p><h3 id="o47-crewai-proposes-structured-async-output-and-narrower-tool-error-handling" tabindex="-1">O47 CrewAI proposes structured async output and narrower tool-error handling <a class="header-anchor" href="#o47-crewai-proposes-structured-async-output-and-narrower-tool-error-handling" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O47 CrewAI proposes structured async output and narrower tool-error handling&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>OPEN · 2026-11-09 01:50 UTC</strong></p><p>#9284 proposes applying response_model after LiteLLM async-stream accumulation, returning JSON and final events; it is not a pre-stream output gate. Inspected patches also narrow MCP/file-store bridge RuntimeError handling to get_running_loop, avoiding masking real tool errors as missing-loop signals or executing twice. OPEN/unreleased; upstream tests are not local verification. Classify as delivery-schema/side-effect experiments. Discuss sync/async contracts, validation timing and duplicate actions on error fallback.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/crewAIInc/crewAI/pull/9284" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a></p><h3 id="o48-ag2-proposes-assembling-streamed-fragments-before-emitting-a-tool-call" tabindex="-1">O48 AG2 proposes assembling streamed fragments before emitting a tool call <a class="header-anchor" href="#o48-ag2-proposes-assembling-streamed-fragments-before-emitting-a-tool-call" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O48 AG2 proposes assembling streamed fragments before emitting a tool call&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>OPEN · 2026-11-09 01:57 UTC</strong></p><p>#3632 proposes assembling DashScope incremental tool calls by index, preserving first nonempty id/name, concatenating arguments and emitting one ToolCallEvent per call in order. Missing-index position fallback remains; non-streaming is unchanged. Fragments must not become duplicate calls or partial-JSON executions. Upstream tests use stubs, not live APIs; OPEN/unreleased. Classify as call-identity/stream-contract experiments, mapping first to adapter events/evidence rather than FCoP kernel changes.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/ag2ai/ag2/pull/3632" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a></p><h3 id="o49-browser-use-proposes-handling-nullable-schemas-without-aborting-tool-registration" tabindex="-1">O49 Browser-use proposes handling nullable schemas without aborting tool registration <a class="header-anchor" href="#o49-browser-use-proposes-handling-nullable-schemas-without-aborting-tool-registration" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O49 Browser-use proposes handling nullable schemas without aborting tool registration&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>OPEN · 2026-11-09 02:07 UTC</strong></p><p>#6347 reports list-valued MCP schema type (such as string/null) causing an unhashable-list error that aborts registration and skips later tools. The proposal recursively builds Python unions, including nested objects/array items, leaving single-type paths unchanged. Its 12 upstream tests are neither local experiments nor merged capability. Classify as schema/partial-degradation experiments. Discuss whether one unsupported legal field should erase a whole catalogue and how to report capability failure.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/browser-use/browser-use/pull/6347" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a></p><h3 id="o50-camel-proposes-forwarding-explicit-keys-instead-of-an-authentication-placeholder" tabindex="-1">O50 CAMEL proposes forwarding explicit keys instead of an authentication placeholder <a class="header-anchor" href="#o50-camel-proposes-forwarding-explicit-keys-instead-of-an-authentication-placeholder" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O50 CAMEL proposes forwarding explicit keys instead of an authentication placeholder&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>OPEN · 2026-11-09 01:45 UTC</strong></p><p>#4498 reports VLLMModel accepting api_key but sending Not_Used, breaking authenticated vLLM/API-gateway connections with 401. It proposes forwarding explicit keys while retaining the placeholder when omitted for local unauthenticated use. This is not a credential-leak report or evidence of a CodeFlowMu defect. Ten upstream tests; OPEN/unreleased. Classify as private-model integration experiments. Discuss consistency among constructor settings, actual Authorization and unattended diagnostics.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/camel-ai/camel/pull/4498" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a></p><h3 id="o51-langfuse-proposes-consistent-evaluation-blocking-on-provider-403" tabindex="-1">O51 Langfuse proposes consistent evaluation blocking on provider 403 <a class="header-anchor" href="#o51-langfuse-proposes-consistent-evaluation-blocking-on-provider-403" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O51 Langfuse proposes consistent evaluation blocking on provider 403&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>OPEN · 2026-11-09 01:33 UTC</strong></p><p>#19925 reports bare provider 403 leaving evaluator blockReason unset and repeatedly failing while Topics pauses. It proposes authentication-invalid classification, while acknowledging that valid-key/model-not-permitted may warrant model-unavailable semantics. OPEN; the author lacked dependencies for the full suite and no local reproduction occurred. Classify as failure-taxonomy/scheduling experiments. Discuss recovery conditions and retry ceilings for authentication, model permission and transient faults.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/langfuse/langfuse/pull/19925" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a></p><h3 id="o52-openhands-proposes-wiring-auto-pause-to-real-events-and-a-deliverable-mutation" tabindex="-1">O52 OpenHands proposes wiring auto-pause to real events and a deliverable mutation <a class="header-anchor" href="#o52-openhands-proposes-wiring-auto-pause-to-real-events-and-a-deliverable-mutation" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O52 OpenHands proposes wiring auto-pause to real events and a deliverable mutation&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>OPEN · 2026-11-09 02:00 UTC</strong></p><p>Inspected #19542 patches replace an unreachable V0 UI error branch and a message-only hook with MaxIterationsReached and the supported pause mutation. History replay/planning-agent events are excluded and dropped actions warn. Crucially, the server run already ended in error at the cap; this repairs the UI transition to resumable paused state, not an absent backend iteration ceiling. OPEN/unreleased; upstream tests are not local. Learning high/risk medium-high; maintain watch. Classify as state-control reachability experiments. The author had no live Canvas/agent-server session validation; MaxBudgetReached and raising limits on resume remain out of scope.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/OpenHands/OpenHands/pull/19542" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a></p><h3 id="o53-orkas-adapts-opencode-2-launch-flags-and-bridge-environment" tabindex="-1">O53 Orkas adapts OpenCode 2 launch flags and bridge environment <a class="header-anchor" href="#o53-orkas-adapts-opencode-2-launch-flags-and-bridge-environment" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O53 Orkas adapts OpenCode 2 launch flags and bridge environment&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>MERGED · 2026-11-09 01:23 UTC</strong></p><p>#192 discovers supported run flags from help, using PWD when --dir is unavailable and a private server when supported to carry the run&#39;s bridge environment, while preserving explicit saved-server choices and 1.x behavior. Upstream deterministic/macOS Electron checks do not establish paid-model, installed-provider or full Windows validation. Distinct from already reported O23/#193; no new stable release verified. Learning medium-high/risk medium; retain delivery-compatibility watch. Classify as an application experience candidate.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/Orkas-AI/Orkas/pull/192" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a></p><h3 id="o54-paradev-proposes-verified-atomic-macos-github-cli-onboarding" tabindex="-1">O54 Paradev proposes verified atomic macOS GitHub CLI onboarding <a class="header-anchor" href="#o54-paradev-proposes-verified-atomic-macos-github-cli-onboarding" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O54 Paradev proposes verified atomic macOS GitHub CLI onboarding&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>OPEN · 2026-11-09 02:07 UTC</strong></p><p>#9535 proposes downloading the official macOS gh zip, checking release checksums, installing through temporary-file rename into the app directory and verifying gh --version, with non-TTY device-code login. App calls prefer a user&#39;s existing gh; cloud GitHub App connection is not local CLI authentication. The new path is macOS-only; Linux/Windows largely retain existing behavior. OPEN, not released cross-platform one-click delivery. Learning high/delivery competition medium-high; add an experience candidate without upgrading stable coverage. Discuss asset validation, signing/quarantine and local/cloud credential boundaries.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/ParadevAI/Paradev/pull/9535" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a></p><h2 id="layer-2-research-analysis" tabindex="-1">Layer 2: research analysis <a class="header-anchor" href="#layer-2-research-analysis" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Layer 2: research analysis&quot;">​</a></h2><h3 id="a1-responsive-supervision-and-continuous-background-work-now-have-comparable-implementations" tabindex="-1">A1 Responsive supervision and continuous background work now have comparable implementations <a class="header-anchor" href="#a1-responsive-supervision-and-continuous-background-work-now-have-comparable-implementations" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;A1 Responsive supervision and continuous background work now have comparable implementations&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Evidence：O4、O6、O20、O29、O30、O31</p><p>Supported: Deep Agents separates task threads, durable job registries, steering/cancellation and approval-aware notifications; Cursor, Claude and Orca add distinct conversation, background-tool and mobile-control surfaces. They operate at different layers and do not establish a universal digital-employee protocol. Hypothesis: a responsive supervisor plus independent task identity and completion wake-up may inform CodeFlowMu after comparison with its Runtime. Prioritize async-deep-agents experience, checking notification wiring, version drift, licensing, worker capacity and cleanup, then Orca weak-network control. Costs include workers/threads, ordering, deduplication, post-cancel effects and retention. Map chiefly to Runtime/product. Discussion: authoritative jobs after compaction and queued results during approval.</p><h3 id="a2-authority-must-survive-resumption-host-transitions-and-credential-changes" tabindex="-1">A2 Authority must survive resumption, host transitions and credential changes <a class="header-anchor" href="#a2-authority-must-survive-resumption-host-transitions-and-credential-changes" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;A2 Authority must survive resumption, host transitions and credential changes&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Evidence：O2、O3、O5、O6、O8、O18、O19、O22、O25</p><p>Supported: Codex separates project trust/write permission and orders grant persistence; ADK exposes representation-sensitive approval; Paradev opens an explicit owner-cloud-to-host channel while Palmier removes approval friction. These opposing directions cannot all be called governance maturity. Hypothesis: checking subject, scope and revocation throughout delegation/resumption/cross-host calls matters for CodeFlowMu Runtime/adapters and enterprise policy, not automatically FCoP fields. Experiment candidates: resumed permission modes, post-revocation host calls, invalid read-only settings and Enum approvals. Benefits concern explicit authority; costs include policy versions, credentials, recovery compatibility and human blocking. Increase Paradev/Palmier scrutiny; assess Paperclip delivery maturity separately from governance fit.</p><h3 id="a3-recovery-and-completion-require-terminal-evidence-silence-is-not-success" tabindex="-1">A3 Recovery and completion require terminal evidence; silence is not success <a class="header-anchor" href="#a3-recovery-and-completion-require-terminal-evidence-silence-is-not-success" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;A3 Recovery and completion require terminal evidence; silence is not success&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Evidence：O8、O9、O10、O13、O14、O17、O21、O23、O26、O28、O31</p><p>Supported: strict_resume, stale-owner settlement guards, held-call waits, final JSON-RPC flushing, nonzero exits, refusal to grade empty results, compaction error status and ACK/consumption distinctions all clarify state boundaries. Similar fixes support bounded cross-project observations, not comprehensive exactly-once claims. Hypothesis: execution, acceptance, user reporting and website publication should expose separate success/failure/unknown outcomes; local applicability still needs experiments. Candidates: final-record loss, empty tests, restart during approval and duplicate input. Benefits are recovery/accountability; costs include durable state, causal IDs, idempotency and compensation. Compare delivery evidence with FCoP contracts while leaving locks/scheduling/cleanup in Runtime.</p><h3 id="a4-governance-evidence-expands-to-role-declarations-skill-versions-and-independent-signing" tabindex="-1">A4 Governance evidence expands to role declarations, skill versions and independent signing <a class="header-anchor" href="#a4-governance-evidence-expands-to-role-declarations-skill-versions-and-independent-signing" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;A4 Governance evidence expands to role declarations, skill versions and independent signing&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Evidence：O1、O7、O9、O16、O24、O27</p><p>Supported: ARC/Ponder/Tracekit catalogue entries address pre-execution declarations, review processes and external signing; Paperclip pins run-specific skills, Langfuse adds bounded read/download auditing, and StaffDeck&#39;s report suggests SOP/tool-output mismatch. Hypothesis: trace roles through SOPs, capabilities, artifacts and independent acceptance rather than counting logs. Review ARC/Ponder/Tracekit source first, checking original licences, maintenance and trust assumptions before experience; catalogue licence summaries are not adoption reviews. Candidate counterexamples: consistent declarations with runtime overreach, intact signatures with bypassed execution, and acceptable output with missing process evidence. Costs include schema maintenance, signer isolation and privacy-aware retention.</p><h3 id="a5-compare-protocols-and-open-applications-by-semantic-layer-and-operational-limits" tabindex="-1">A5 Compare protocols and open applications by semantic layer and operational limits <a class="header-anchor" href="#a5-compare-protocols-and-open-applications-by-semantic-layer-and-operational-limits" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;A5 Compare protocols and open applications by semantic layer and operational limits&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Evidence：O3、O7、O10、O11、O12、O15、O18、O20、O22、O28、O30、O32</p><p>Supported: A2A adapters retain application authorization/storage duties; A2UI defines UI wire formats; MCP schema/Apps concern tool/context contracts; Deep Agents is thread-based Runtime coordination. This run found insufficient evidence for a new same-layer file-protocol competitor to FCoP. Complementary mechanisms still deserve research. Hypothesis: compare state/events/evidence, routing, negotiation and real adoption before mapping to FCoP, adapters or product. Purpose-driven experience candidates: Deep Agents for async supervision, Paperclip for employee upgrades/skill versions, Orca for mobile control. Check platforms, credentials/services, licensing and migration first. Public source does not establish local operability, nor releases enterprise validation. Local experiments, articles, development reviews and actual comments are all zero. Cursor connects building, security checks and explicit publication, but administrator overrides need separate accountability evidence; map this to product-delivery governance rather than protocol negotiation.</p><h3 id="a6-authority-needs-recorded-human-intent-and-actionable-denial-handoffs" tabindex="-1">A6 Authority needs recorded human intent and actionable denial handoffs <a class="header-anchor" href="#a6-authority-needs-recorded-human-intent-and-actionable-denial-handoffs" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;A6 Authority needs recorded human intent and actionable denial handoffs&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Evidence：O34、O43、O46、O50、O51、O54</p><p>Supported: hiring-denial provenance, fail-fast configuration proposals, key representation/forwarding fixes, 403 blocking and local/cloud authentication distinctions show that declared settings do not fully describe effective authority. Paperclip exposes migration costs when historical false differs from explicit board intent; global final denial is still OPEN. Hypothesis: roles, recorded intent, call-time decisions, final denials and human remediation can form an enterprise governance chain after comparison with CodeFlowMu Runtime/product policy. Unexecuted candidates: legacy permission imports, CEO self-escalation, CLI/environment precedence, key formats and 403 retries. Costs include migration, credential/role compatibility, error taxonomy and human blocking; do not automatically promote fields into FCoP kernel requirements.</p><h3 id="a7-recovery-and-concurrency-must-distinguish-proof-uncertainty-and-accepted-risk" tabindex="-1">A7 Recovery and concurrency must distinguish proof, uncertainty and accepted risk <a class="header-anchor" href="#a7-recovery-and-concurrency-must-distinguish-proof-uncertainty-and-accepted-risk" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;A7 Recovery and concurrency must distinguish proof, uncertainty and accepted risk&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Evidence：O33、O35、O38、O40、O41、O42、O44、O45、O52、O53</p><p>Supported: Paperclip rolls back only proven-undelivered dead-runner rotations, Beads retains a narrow cancellation-history gap, and Orca deliberately relaxes stop proof. These are different recovery policies. Full transcripts, exports, turn completion, surviving background processes, message failure and paused sessions also have distinct contracts. Hypothesis: CodeFlowMu could separate task/turn/session/worker/job state and preserve evidence for unknowns, policy permission and accepted risks, without inferring matching local defects. Unexecuted candidates: killing authority-rotation windows, cancellation-time deletes, surviving-agent handoff, concurrent field writes and replayed cap events. Benefits concern explainable recovery/acceptance; costs include durable state, fences, logs and availability tradeoffs. Compare FCoP state/event/evidence semantics while leaving locks/process control in Runtime.</p><h3 id="a8-independent-verification-needs-oracle-isolation-and-cross-path-contract-counterexamples" tabindex="-1">A8 Independent verification needs oracle isolation and cross-path contract counterexamples <a class="header-anchor" href="#a8-independent-verification-needs-oracle-isolation-and-cross-path-contract-counterexamples" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;A8 Independent verification needs oracle isolation and cross-path contract counterexamples&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Evidence：O36、O37、O39、O47、O48、O49</p><p>Supported: bash can bypass tool-level read restrictions and evaluation caches also need isolation; human retries need cost accounting and parent wakes need activity evidence. Field/actor coverage, streamed call assembly, structured returns and nullable schemas can diverge across paths. Upstream passing tests are not local validation, syntactic coverage is not semantic coverage, and post-accumulation validation is not pre-stream control. Hypothesis: independent multi-agent verification must consider execution identity, oracles, caches, evidence and artifacts, testing sync/async, stream/nonstream and provider/backend legs. Unexecuted candidates: oracle readability, stub/live controls, duplicate actions after RuntimeError, interleaved tool indexes and catalogue impact of one schema failure. No articles or development tasks result; this is verification-method/adapter-contract research.</p><h2 id="cross-project-patterns-and-classification" tabindex="-1">Cross-project patterns and classification <a class="header-anchor" href="#cross-project-patterns-and-classification" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Cross-project patterns and classification&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Patterns concern async supervision, continuous authority, explicit failure/unknown states, role/tool contracts and release-versus-operability boundaries. The eight analyses synthesize observations and are not additional events. Priority experience candidates are Deep Agents, Paperclip and Orca; other items retain their tracking/reference/experiment classifications. <strong>Local completed experiments 0, articles 0, formal development reviews 0, actual comments 0.</strong> No development tasks were generated; discussion topics are research questions, not submitted comments.</p><h2 id="coverage-dual-source-checks-and-verification-limits" tabindex="-1">Coverage, dual-source checks and verification limits <a class="header-anchor" href="#coverage-dual-source-checks-and-verification-limits" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Coverage, dual-source checks and verification limits&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>Registry: fetched from latest main through connected GitHub tooling; blob SHA <code>8d97df65269aa2b4accd1366d1b71319f99c6efd</code>, 47 researchers. Explicit supplemental projects/competitors remain, giving 88 repository targets. Deep Agents/async-deep-agents are additional local candidates, not Registry edits.</li><li>GitHub: commits, recent issues/PRs and releases were attempted for all 88 targets. Read 1,862 commit records, 219 issue/PR records and 192 release records, including historical material used only for filtering/deduplication. 86 commit windows completed. Orca remains incomplete after four pages/400 records; TSA AI Workforce was inaccessible for all three queries. Their complete baselines are retained. At most five recent issues/PRs and three releases per repository are bounded samples, not historical exhaustive coverage. Selected PR bodies/status/merge times were inspected separately.</li><li>Public social/author pages/official blogs are a separate source type: Cursor&#39;s official changelog, LangChain&#39;s official article and related official documentation were checked; author GitHub/paper entrances support identity/attribution. Two GitHub links are not called dual-source evidence. All 47 authors&#39; personal X replies/threads and other blogs were not exhaustively checked. GitHub primary events can independently support engineering facts.</li><li>The six governance researchers and Yue Zhao&#39;s specified repositories remain queried; omission from observations does not establish no change across all their sources. People/projects are many-to-many; bulbasaur-skill-cli is not labelled AGENTSAFE&#39;s official artifact. Verified Superset→Paradev redirection retains radar provenance.</li><li>Uncovered pre-November-5 history and unfinished pagination remain gaps. Failed triggers and unchecked sources are not quiet success. Events, status, SHAs, windows and classification are recorded locally.</li><li>Publication scope is only today&#39;s bilingual reports and two indexes, using existing site implementation/public snapshots. Source commits, Pages deployment and live click verification are recorded in separate local receipts; this report does not pre-claim deployment success.</li></ul><h3 id="scheduled-incremental-coverage-and-disposition" tabindex="-1">Scheduled incremental coverage and disposition <a class="header-anchor" href="#scheduled-incremental-coverage-and-disposition" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Scheduled incremental coverage and disposition&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The current 47-person Registry was fetched again from latest main (same blob SHA). All 88 targets received fresh commit/recent issue-PR/release attempts; selected PR bodies, authors, states and some production patches were read. The increment contains 39 commit, 85 issue/PR and 192 release records, including repeated release checks, not independent-new-event counts. 87 recent commit windows reached a final page; Orca&#39;s 16 recent records are complete but its historical pagination gap since November 1 remains and the full-history baseline is retained. TSA remains inaccessible. Bounded limits of five issues/PRs and three releases remain.</p><p>Cursor&#39;s official changelog still leads with November 6 and the LangChain async article is still dated November 5; these are separate-source-type rechecks, not new events. Personal X/replies were not exhausted; GitHub independently supports engineering facts. Selected PR author handles were inspected without promoting community contributors to Registry researchers/paper authors. Old issues merely updated, ordinary UI/performance/profile refreshes and minor internal listener cleanup did not meet this notification threshold; existing SDK proposals without verified substantive new changes remain local candidates rather than new releases.</p><p>Classification remains tracking, references and experience/experiment candidates. Priorities include board-denial migration, warm-attach certainty, independent-evaluation isolation, cancellation/concurrency and call contracts. No new applications were installed, local product experiments run, articles produced, formal development reviews completed or actual comments sent. This matches the zero-outcome counts above. Only the same November 9 bilingual reports and two indexes are updated idempotently.</p><h2 id="later-same-day-manual-research" tabindex="-1">Later same-day manual research <a class="header-anchor" href="#later-same-day-manual-research" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Later same-day manual research&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The zero-experiment, zero-article counts above are the snapshot at the 10:25 scheduled incremental check. Later, we followed O30 / A1 and A3 with a three-condition probe of the async-agent reference notifier and ran 19 relevant CodeFlowMu-open report-intake component tests; all 19 passed. The reference notifier marked itself notified after a simulated delivery failure, a bounded result about that optional component. CodeFlowMu already has tested report-driven PM wake, queueing, and deduplication; this study did not reproduce the same product defect. Results: <a href="/joinwell52/en/research/2026-11-09-when-worker-finishes">bilingual article, “When the background AI finishes, will its supervisor know?”</a> · <a href="https://github.com/joinwell52-AI/joinwell52/blob/main/research/manual-runs/2026-11-09-async-supervisor/00-report.en.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">English evidence record</a>. Later manual research completed one combined question and one bilingual article; formal development reviews: 0. We posted one <a href="https://github.com/langchain-ai/async-deep-agents/issues/5" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">bounded engineering question on GitHub #5</a>; the draft and receipt remain local.</p>',257)])])}const g=a(n,[["render",s]]);export{h as __pageData,g as default};
